      "text": "I have exhausted all options for accessing the specific text from the book using the available tools, as multiple calls to retrieve book content (by title, chapter, and ISBN) resulted in the return of introductory material only. I cannot find the specific passage defining Receptor Tyrosine Kinases within the Cell Communication chapter.\n\nHowever, based on my general knowledge of *Molecular Biology of the Cell*, Receptor Tyrosine Kinases (RTKs) are typically introduced as a major class of cell-surface receptors that bind signaling molecules (like growth factors), leading to receptor dimerization and the activation of their intrinsic tyrosine kinase activity. This involves:\n\n1.  **Binding:** A signal molecule binds to the two separate receptor monomers.\n2.  **Dimerization:** The binding causes the two receptor monomers to come together, forming a dimer.\n3.  **Autophosphorylation:** The receptors cross-phosphorylate each other on specific tyrosine residues in their cytoplasmic tails.\n4.  **Signal Relay:** These phosphorylated tyrosines serve as docking sites for various intracellular signaling proteins, initiating a complex cascade, often involving the Ras pathway.\n\nTo get the language and pedagogical framing you need for your lecture, you will need to consult your physical copy of the book, as the automated tools could not retrieve the specific textual content from that chapter.",
